window.docContents[161].resolve({"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L3-___-The-body-opens-up--Small-rigorous-improvement":{"contents":"* Question* Context change* Attribute change* Risk shown* PhysLib stage\n* Body A wobbles instead of sliding along the line. Why, and what must the model now say about the body?* Body A of pair P becomes a rigid composite carved into left and right halves; a body-fixed frame appears beside the lab frame.* Per-half mass and position, centre of mass, inertia tensor, and body- versus lab-frame angular velocity.* Counting the wrong halves changes total mass; angular velocity is intensive; a massless body's centre of mass evaluates to the origin, which is not physical; lab-frame angular velocity does not match the body-frame energy input.* Stages 2–3 on rigid bodies; proved solid-sphere inertia theorem.\n\nThe L2 on-axis point-mass assumption has fallen. L4 revisits the remaining assumption of\nmotion along one line seen by one observer.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L3 — The body opens up","header":"5.4.5. Small rigorous improvement","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L3-___-The-body-opens-up--Small-rigorous-improvement"},"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Standards-Reference-___-PKC-beside-ISO-80000-1-and-the-VIM--Kinds-of-property":{"contents":"* This work* Dybkær (2009)* ISO 80000-1:2022* VIM 3 (JCGM 200:2012)* VIM 4 2CD* KindOfProperty* §6.19* 4.1 general quantity* 1.2 kind of quantity* 1.1 Note 1 general quantity (no entry)* KindOfProperty.IsQuantity* §13.3.1* 4.1 quantity* 1.1 quantity* 1.1 quantity* KindOfProperty.IsNominal* §13.2.1* Clause 1 (nominal properties, not covered)* 1.30 nominal property* 6.1 nominal property* KindOfProperty.IsOrdinal* §13.2.2* Clause 1 (ordinal quantities, not covered)* 1.26 ordinal quantity* 1.33 ordinal quantity* KindOfProperty.IsDifferential* §13.2.3* —* —* 1.4 interval quantity* KindOfProperty.IsRational* §13.2.4* 4.1 (comparable by ratio)* —* 1.3 ratio quantity* KindOfProperty.nominal_not_quantity* §13.3.1* Clause 1; 4.1* 1.30 nominal property* 1.1 Note 3; 6.1 nominal property* IndividualProperty* no section given* 4.1 individual quantity* 1.1 Note 1 (individual quantities)* 1.1 Notes 1–2 individual quantity (no entry)\n\n* Agreement. IsQuantity holds of exactly the ordinal, interval and ratio kinds. All\nfour sources count the ordinal level as quantity and the nominal level as not, which is\nthe content of nominal_not_quantity.* KindOfProperty is broader than kind of quantity. The VIM 3 term\ncovers quantities only. Following Dybkær §6.19, a KindOfProperty may also be nominal,\nso it is the common genus of kind of quantity and of the kind of a nominal property.\nThe ISO/VIM kind of quantity corresponds to a KindOfProperty for which IsQuantity\nholds.* Identity of a kind. VIM 3 defines a kind as the aspect common to mutually comparable\nquantities and notes that the division into kinds is to some extent arbitrary. A\nKindOfProperty is a declared value with an identifier, a scale type and an optional\nexamination principle (Dybkær §7.5), and two kinds are the same exactly when these\nthree agree. Two kinds that share a dimension are therefore distinct unless declared\nequal, which makes formal the remark of VIM 3 1.2 Note 2 and VIM 4 2CD 1.2 Note 3 that\nquantities of the same dimension are not necessarily of the same kind. ISO\n80000-1:2022 clause 5 states only the other direction: quantities of the same kind\nhave the same dimension, and quantities of different dimensions are of different\nkinds.* ISO 80000-1:2022 says general quantity. Clause 4.1 does not use kind of\nquantity; it says that comparable quantities are of the same kind and are instances\nof the same general quantity, as VIM 4 2CD does.* VIM 4 2CD drops the term. It has no entry kind of quantity; it speaks of a\ngeneral quantity in the notes to 1.1 and defines quantities of the same kind (1.2)\ninstead. KindOfProperty corresponds to its general quantity, or general nominal\nproperty.* IndividualProperty is the individual quantity of the notes to 1.1, widened in the\nsame way to nominal kinds. ISO 80000-1:2022 4.1 names the individual quantity in its\ntext; none of the ISO or VIM documents gives it an entry of its own, and the Lean source cites no Dybkær section for it.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Standards Reference — PKC beside ISO 80000-1 and the VIM","header":"34.3. Kinds of property","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Standards-Reference-___-PKC-beside-ISO-80000-1-and-the-VIM--Kinds-of-property"}});